---
Part III, Chapter 4: The document builder
Package: packages/loom-lang/src/ast/LoomAstBuilder.ts
Language: TypeScript
---

# The document builder

`LoomAstBuilder` is the final parse stage: `build` turns a stream of wefts into a `LoomDocument`. A weft before the first heading stands at the document's top level, as frontmatter or preamble; a heading gathers the body wefts up to the next heading into a section. `build` groups the wefts along that line, then folds each group into the document. The module also holds `emptyDocument`, the empty error-carrying tree a caller falls back to when it cannot produce a real one.

## The build

Grouping a heading with its body means looking ahead to the next heading, so `build` gathers the whole weft stream first with `Stream.runCollect`. `faultUnclosedFence` first marks fenced code that never closes, `faultUnclosedBox` a box that never closes, `warnHeadingAgainstCode` a heading that most likely belonged to the code above it, and `warnDividerAgainstCode` a divider that most likely did. `groupNodes` then splits the wefts into the document's top-level nodes, `appendToDocument` folds each node into a growing document, and `makeDocument` finishes it.

=>

export class LoomAstBuilder extends Context.Service<LoomAstBuilder>()('LoomAstBuilder', {
  make: Effect.succeed({
    build: (source: Stream.Stream<LoomWeft>): Effect.Effect<LoomDocument> =>
      Stream.runCollect(source).pipe(
        Effect.map((wefts) =>
          makeDocument(
            Array.reduce(
              groupNodes(
                warnDividerAgainstCode(
                  warnHeadingAgainstCode(faultUnclosedBox(faultUnclosedFence(wefts))),
                ),
              ),
              initialDocument,
              appendToDocument,
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

## Fenced code that never closes

Fenced code holds its lines until a fence that matches the one that opened it. The classifier honours that line by line, so when no closing fence comes, every line to the end of the file reads as code, headings included. Nothing in the classifier can say so, because it has already emitted the opening fence by the time the file runs out. The builder sees the whole weft list at once, so the check lives here.

Only the last fenced code can be open. Inside fenced code every line is code, so no second fence can open while one is open. `unclosedFence` finds the last fence weft and keeps it only when it opens. `faultUnclosedFence` then gives that fence's backticks an `UnclosedFence` fault. The fault is an error, so a tangle refuses the file rather than writing a section that swallowed the rest of it, and the editor underlines the fence where the code began.

=>

const isFence = (weft: LoomWeft): weft is FenceWeft => weft.type === 'FenceWeft'

const opensFence = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean => isFence(weft) && weft.side === 'open'

const unclosedFence = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): Option.Option<number> =>
  pipe(
    Array.findLastIndex(wefts, isFence),
    Option.filter((at) => Option.exists(Array.get(wefts, at), opensFence)),
  )

const faultedFence = (weft: FenceWeft): FenceWeft => ({
  ...weft,
  fence: {
    ...weft.fence,
    health: faulty(UnclosedFence({ fence: weft.fence.source }), weft.fence.position),
  },
})

const faultUnclosedFence = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  Option.match(unclosedFence(wefts), {
    onNone: () => wefts,
    onSome: (open) =>
      Array.map(wefts, (weft, at) => (at === open && isFence(weft) ? faultedFence(weft) : weft)),
  })

## A box that never closes

A box holds its lines until a line of three colons alone. The classifier honours that line by line, so when no such line comes, every line to the end of the file reads as the box's body, headings included. As with fenced code, only the builder sees the whole weft list, so the check lives here too.

Only the last box can be open, since a box never opens inside another. `unclosedBox` finds the last line that opens or closes a box, and keeps it only when that line opens one. A stray opens and closes nothing, so `isBoxEdge` passes it by. `faultUnclosedBox` then gives the opening line an `UnclosedBox` fault through `faultedBoxLine`, which places a fault on a box line. The fault is an error, so a tangle refuses the file rather than lose the sections the box swallowed.

=>

const isBoxEdge = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  Option.exists(boxOf(weft), (box) => box.side !== 'stray')

const opensBox = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  Option.exists(boxOf(weft), (box) => box.side === 'open')

const unclosedBox = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): Option.Option<number> =>
  pipe(
    Array.findLastIndex(wefts, isBoxEdge),
    Option.filter((at) => Option.exists(Array.get(wefts, at), opensBox)),
  )

const faultedBox =
  (fault: LoomFault) =>
  <Line extends PreambleWeft | ProseWeft>(line: Line): Line =>
    Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(line.box), {
      onNone: () => line,
      onSome: (box) => ({
        ...line,
        box: { ...box, fence: { ...box.fence, health: faulty(fault, box.position) } },
      }),
    })

const faultedBoxLine =
  (fault: LoomFault) =>
  (weft: LoomWeft): LoomWeft =>
    Match.value(weft).pipe(
      Match.when({ type: 'PreambleWeft' }, faultedBox(fault)),
      Match.when({ type: 'ProseWeft' }, faultedBox(fault)),
      Match.orElse(() => weft),
    )

const faultUnclosedBox = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  Option.match(unclosedBox(wefts), {
    onNone: () => wefts,
    onSome: (open) =>
      Array.map(wefts, (weft, at) => (at === open ? faultedBoxLine(UnclosedBox())(weft) : weft)),
  })

## A box that points to a loom

A box of the `Chapter` kind points to the chapter its name titles, and a box of the `Discussion` kind points to the discussion its name titles. A box of the `Trope` kind names a trope, which its `anchors` key points to the section or chapter that holds it. `judgeLoomBoxes` reads a section's body, and `loomBoxFault` gives each such box there the fault it earns. A box of any of the three kinds with no name points nowhere, or names nothing, so it earns an `UnnamedBox` fault that names its kind.

A chapter box marks the place where a thought leaves its section for a chapter of its own, so the code of that section belongs in the chapter. A chapter box in a section that holds code therefore earns a `ChapterBoxBesideCode` fault, and a section holds code when an arrow opens some in it. A discussion box may stand beside code, since a discussion often argues about the code beside it. Both faults are errors, and `faultedBoxLine` places each on its box line.

=>

const loomBoxFault = (box: BoxToken, code: boolean): Option.Option<LoomFault> => {
  const kind = boxKind(box)
  return box.side !== 'open' || !isPointingKind(kind)
    ? Option.none()
    : Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(box.name), {
        onNone: () => Option.some(UnnamedBox({ kind })),
        onSome: (name) =>
          code && kind === chapterKind
            ? Option.some(ChapterBoxBesideCode({ name: name.value }))
            : Option.none(),
      })
}

const judgeLoomBoxes = (body: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> => {
  const code = Array.some(body, (weft) => weft.type === 'ArrowWeft')
  return Array.map(body, (weft) =>
    Option.match(
      Option.flatMap(boxOf(weft), (box) => loomBoxFault(box, code)),
      {
        onNone: () => weft,
        onSome: (fault) => faultedBoxLine(fault)(weft),
      },
    ),
  )
}

## A conclusion closes a topic

A discussion divides its thread into topics, and a divider opens each one, as the chapter "Discussions" describes. A topic closes with at most one box of the `Conclusion` kind, the answer the topic settled on. `judgeConclusions` reads a section's body for its conclusions. A conclusion anywhere but in a topic, a section that a divider opens in a discussion, earns a `ConclusionOutsideTopic` fault. In a topic, every conclusion after the first earns a `SecondConclusion` fault. `isConclusion` tells the line that opens a conclusion, and `conclusionFault` decides each one's fault from whether it stands in a topic and how many came before it.

=>

const isConclusion = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  Option.exists(boxOf(weft), (box) => box.side === 'open' && boxKind(box) === conclusionKind)

const conclusionFault = (topic: boolean, earlier: number): Option.Option<LoomFault> =>
  Match.value({ topic, earlier }).pipe(
    Match.when({ topic: false }, () => Option.some(ConclusionOutsideTopic())),
    Match.when({ earlier: 0 }, () => Option.none()),
    Match.orElse(() => Option.some(SecondConclusion())),
  )

const judgeConclusions = (body: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>, topic: boolean): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  Array.mapAccum(body, 0, (earlier, weft) =>
    isConclusion(weft)
      ? [
          earlier + 1,
          Option.match(conclusionFault(topic, earlier), {
            onNone: () => weft,
            onSome: (fault) => faultedBoxLine(fault)(weft),
          }),
        ]
      : [earlier, weft],
  )[1]

## A heading against the code

An arrow holds its code until the next heading. A `#` comment in a script, or a heading inside a Markdown file, reads as that heading, and the code stops short without a word. Fencing the code is the cure, but an author has to know to reach for it.

The tell is the blank line. A real heading has one above it, and a comment usually sits flush against the code it follows. So a heading whose previous weft is a non-blank code line gets a `HeadingAgainstCode` warning on its marker. A warning leaves the tangle running, because a heading flush against code may be what the author meant. It still reaches the editor, at the line where the code was cut.

=>

const isFlushCode = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  weft.type === 'CodeWeft' && weft.source.trim() !== ''

const warnedHeading = (weft: HeadingWeft): HeadingWeft => ({
  ...weft,
  headingStart: {
    ...weft.headingStart,
    health: faulty(HeadingAgainstCode(), weft.headingStart.position),
  },
})

const warnHeadingAgainstCode = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  Array.map(wefts, (weft, at) =>
    isHeading(weft) && Option.exists(Array.get(wefts, at - 1), isFlushCode)
      ? warnedHeading(weft)
      : weft,
  )

~

A divider ends code just as a heading does, and code holds lines of dashes of its own more often than lines that start with `#`. A YAML file separates its documents with three dashes, a Lua doc comment opens with them, and a Markdown file underlines a heading with them. So a divider directly under a line of code, or directly under the arrow that opens code, gets a `DividerAgainstCode` warning on its dashes. Such dashes are no good loom either way: the cure is to fence the code, which the formatter does, as the chapter "Formatting a loom" describes.

=>

const dividesCode = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean => isFlushCode(weft) || weft.type === 'ArrowWeft'

const warnedDivider = (weft: DividerWeft): DividerWeft => ({
  ...weft,
  dashes: {
    ...weft.dashes,
    health: faulty(DividerAgainstCode(), weft.dashes.position),
  },
})

const warnDividerAgainstCode = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  Array.map(wefts, (weft, at) =>
    isDivider(weft) && Option.exists(Array.get(wefts, at - 1), dividesCode)
      ? warnedDivider(weft)
      : weft,
  )

## Grouping the wefts

`groupNodes` splits the flat weft list at the first heading. What comes before stands at the document's top level — its frontmatter and preamble — so `isTopLevelWeft` keeps those two kinds. From the first heading on, `Array.chop` walks the rest one opener at a time: each `sectionChunk` takes an opener and the body wefts up to the next one, and `buildSection` turns them into a `LoomSection`. The `isHeading`, `isDivider`, `isSectionBody`, `isPreamble`, and `isToc` predicates classify each weft.

An opener is a heading or a division, and `Opener` names the two. A division opens with a divider. When the line directly above the divider is a line of prose, that line labels the division, so the label opens the chunk together with its divider and no longer counts as prose of the section above. `isLabelLine` tells such a line: a preamble or prose line with words on it, which is no box line and no fence. `labelsAt` tells a label from the divider below it, and `opensSection` tells where the next chunk begins. `openerOf` reads the opener at the head of a chunk, with the number of lines it spans, one for a heading or a bare divider and two for a labelled one. A chunk always begins where the previous one ended, at an opener, so `openerOf` finds one in a well-formed file. `namesDiscussion` tells from the frontmatter whether the loom is a discussion, which every chunk is told, since a division of a discussion is a topic.

=>

const isHeading = (w: LoomWeft): w is HeadingWeft => w.type === 'HeadingWeft'

const isDivider = (w: LoomWeft): w is DividerWeft => w.type === 'DividerWeft'

const isSectionBody = (w: LoomWeft): w is SectionBodyWeft =>
  w.type === 'ArrowWeft' ||
  w.type === 'CodeWeft' ||
  w.type === 'FenceWeft' ||
  w.type === 'TildeWeft' ||
  w.type === 'ProseWeft'

const isPreamble = (w: LoomWeft): w is PreambleWeft => w.type === 'PreambleWeft'

const isToc = (w: LoomWeft): w is TocWeft => w.type === 'TocWeft'

const isTopLevelWeft = (w: LoomWeft): w is FrontmatterWeft | PreambleWeft =>
  w.type === 'FrontmatterWeft' || w.type === 'PreambleWeft'

type TopLevelNode = FrontmatterWeft | PreambleWeft | LoomSection

type LabelLine = PreambleWeft | ProseWeft

type Opener = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  Heading: { readonly heading: HeadingWeft }
  Division: { readonly divider: DividerWeft; readonly label: Option.Option<LabelLine> }
}>

const Opener = Data.taggedEnum<Opener>()

const isLabelLine = (w: LoomWeft): w is LabelLine =>
  (w.type === 'PreambleWeft' || w.type === 'ProseWeft') &&
  w.source.trim() !== '' &&
  Option.isNone(boxOf(w)) &&
  Option.isNone(openingFence(w.source))

const labelsAt = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>, at: number): boolean =>
  Option.exists(Array.get(wefts, at), isLabelLine) &&
  Option.exists(Array.get(wefts, at + 1), isDivider)

const opensSection = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>, at: number): boolean =>
  Option.exists(
    Array.get(wefts, at),
    (w) => isHeading(w) || labelsAt(wefts, at) || (isDivider(w) && !labelsAt(wefts, at - 1)),
  )

const labelledDivision = (
  group: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>,
): Option.Option<readonly [Opener, number]> =>
  Option.map(
    Option.all([
      Option.filter(Array.get(group, 0), isLabelLine),
      Option.filter(Array.get(group, 1), isDivider),
    ]),
    ([label, divider]) => [Opener.Division({ divider, label: Option.some(label) }), 2] as const,
  )

const openerOf = (
  group: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>,
): Option.Option<readonly [Opener, number]> =>
  Option.orElse(labelledDivision(group), () =>
    Match.value(Array.headNonEmpty(group)).pipe(
      Match.when({ type: 'HeadingWeft' }, (heading) =>
        Option.some([Opener.Heading({ heading }), 1] as const),
      ),
      Match.when({ type: 'DividerWeft' }, (divider) =>
        Option.some([Opener.Division({ divider, label: Option.none() }), 1] as const),
      ),
      Match.orElse(() => Option.none<readonly [Opener, number]>()),
    ),
  )

const sectionChunk =
  (discussion: boolean) =>
  (
    group: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>,
  ): readonly [Option.Option<LoomSection>, ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>] => {
    const opened = openerOf(group)
    const width = Option.match(opened, { onNone: () => 1, onSome: ([, lines]) => lines })
    const end = pipe(
      Array.findFirstIndex(Array.drop(group, width), (_, at) => opensSection(group, at + width)),
      Option.map((at) => at + width),
      Option.getOrElse(() => group.length),
    )
    return [
      Option.map(opened, ([opener]) =>
        buildSection(opener, Array.take(Array.drop(group, width), end - width), discussion),
      ),
      Array.drop(group, end),
    ]
  }

const namesDiscussion = (w: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  w.type === 'FrontmatterWeft' && w.discussion !== undefined

const groupNodes = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<TopLevelNode> => {
  const [preamble, sections] = Array.span(wefts, (w) => !isHeading(w))
  const discussion = Array.some(preamble, namesDiscussion)
  return [
    ...Array.filter(preamble, isTopLevelWeft),
    ...Array.getSomes(Array.chop(sections, sectionChunk(discussion))),
  ]
}

## Folding into the document

The fold routes each peeled node. `appendToDocument` appends a `LoomSection` to the document's sections, a `PreambleWeft` to its preamble, and a `FrontmatterWeft` to its frontmatter, starting from the empty `initialDocument`.

=>

type DocumentBuilder = {
  readonly frontmatter: ReadonlyArray<FrontmatterWeft>
  readonly preamble: ReadonlyArray<PreambleWeft>
  readonly sections: ReadonlyArray<LoomSection>
}

const initialDocument: DocumentBuilder = {
  frontmatter: [],
  preamble: [],
  sections: [],
}

const appendToDocument = (doc: DocumentBuilder, node: TopLevelNode): DocumentBuilder =>
  pipe(
    Match.value(node),
    Match.when({ type: 'LoomSection' }, (section) => ({
      ...doc,
      sections: [...doc.sections, section],
    })),
    Match.when({ type: 'PreambleWeft' }, (weft) => ({
      ...doc,
      preamble: [...doc.preamble, weft],
    })),
    Match.when({ type: 'FrontmatterWeft' }, (weft) => ({
      ...doc,
      frontmatter: [...doc.frontmatter, weft],
    })),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

## Building a section

`buildSection` turns an opener and its body chunk into a `LoomSection`: it judges the boxes that point to a loom and the conclusions in the body, the latter by whether the section is a topic of a discussion, partitions the body into preamble, code, and toc-entry wefts, then derives the section's source and span. Only a `{TOC}` section carries entries; every other section leaves that run empty. `headingOf` lifts an opener to a `LoomHeading`. A heading weft gives its own parts, its run of `#` marks as the marker. A division gives the divider's dashes as the marker and its label as the title, through `divisionHeadingOf`, and its source and span run from the label to the dashes, so no line of the loom goes missing. `titleOfLabel` trims the label's line to the words on it; `sourceOf` concatenates the children's sources to reconstruct the byte range; `spanFrom` runs the span from the heading's start to the last child's end.

=>

const buildSection = (
  opener: Opener,
  body: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>,
  discussion: boolean,
): LoomSection => {
  const h = headingOf(opener)
  const topic = discussion && opener._tag === 'Division'
  const judged = judgeConclusions(judgeLoomBoxes(body), topic)
  const preamble = judged.filter(isPreamble)
  const code = judged.filter(isSectionBody)
  const entries = judged.filter(isToc)
  return LoomSectionSchema.make({
    position: spanFrom(h.position, preamble, code, entries),
    source: sourceOf(h, preamble, code, entries),
    health: okHealth,
    heading: h,
    preamble,
    code,
    entries: entries.length > 0 ? entries : undefined,
  })
}

const headingOf = (opener: Opener): LoomHeading =>
  Opener.$match(opener, {
    Heading: ({ heading }) =>
      LoomHeadingSchema.make({
        position: heading.position,
        source: heading.source,
        health: heading.health,
        unexpected: heading.unexpected,
        marker: heading.headingStart,
        title: heading.title,
        specifier: heading.specifier,
        sink: heading.sink,
      }),
    Division: ({ divider, label }) => divisionHeadingOf(divider, label),
  })

const titleOfLabel = (line: LabelLine): HeadingTitleToken => {
  const content = line.source.replace(/\r?\n$/, '')
  const words = content.trim()
  const start = line.position.start.offset + content.length - content.trimStart().length
  return HeadingTitleTokenSchema.make({
    position: {
      start: { line: line.position.start.line, offset: start },
      end: { line: line.position.start.line, offset: start + words.length },
    },
    source: words,
    health: okHealth,
  })
}

const divisionHeadingOf = (divider: DividerWeft, label: Option.Option<LabelLine>): LoomHeading =>
  LoomHeadingSchema.make({
    position: {
      start: Option.match(label, {
        onNone: () => divider.position.start,
        onSome: (line) => line.position.start,
      }),
      end: divider.position.end,
    },
    source:
      Option.match(label, { onNone: () => '', onSome: (line) => line.source }) + divider.source,
    health: divider.health,
    marker: divider.dashes,
    title: Option.getOrUndefined(Option.map(label, titleOfLabel)),
  })

const sourceOf = (
  ...groups: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly source: string } | ReadonlyArray<{ readonly source: string }>>
): string =>
  pipe(
    groups,
    Array.flatMap((g) => Array.ensure(g)),
    Array.map((n) => n.source),
    Array.join(''),
  )

const spanFrom = (
  headingPos: Position,
  ...groups: ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<{ readonly position: Position }>>
): Position => {
  const flat = groups.flat()
  return {
    start: headingPos.start,
    end: flat.length === 0 ? headingPos.end : flat[flat.length - 1].position.end,
  }
}

## Building the frontmatter

`makeFrontmatter` gathers a file's frontmatter wefts into one `LoomFrontmatter`, or nothing when the file opened with no block. The membership comes from up to two lines — the part from one, the chapter from the other — and each is numbered or not: a part carries a number and a name, or a name alone; a chapter a number and a title, or a title alone, and a discussion the same, with its number in place of a chapter's. The `Trope` field, when a loom belongs to a trope, is read on its own, since it sits beside the membership lines rather than in them. The `Language`, `Status`, and `Priority` fields join the tangle target, each read by name. The target answers to `Target`, or to `Package` — the older spelling it is replacing, still honoured so a file may use either during the move.

=>

const makeFrontmatter = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<FrontmatterWeft>): Option.Option<LoomFrontmatter> => {
  if (wefts.length === 0) return Option.none()
  const partFields = Option.match(
    Option.fromNullishOr(wefts.find((w) => w.part !== undefined || w.partName !== undefined)),
    {
      onNone: () => ({}),
      onSome: (w) => ({ part: w.part, partName: w.partName }),
    },
  )
  const chapterFields = Option.match(
    Option.fromNullishOr(
      wefts.find(
        (w) => w.chapter !== undefined || w.discussion !== undefined || w.title !== undefined,
      ),
    ),
    {
      onNone: () => ({}),
      onSome: (w) => ({ chapter: w.chapter, discussion: w.discussion, title: w.title }),
    },
  )
  return Option.some(
    LoomFrontmatterSchema.make({
      position: {
        start: wefts[0].position.start,
        end: wefts[wefts.length - 1].position.end,
      },
      source: sourceOf(wefts),
      health: okHealth,
      ...partFields,
      ...chapterFields,
      package: Option.getOrUndefined(
        Option.orElse(frontmatterField(wefts, 'Target'), () => frontmatterField(wefts, 'Package')),
      ),
      trope: wefts.find((w) => w.trope !== undefined)?.trope,
      language: Option.getOrUndefined(frontmatterField(wefts, 'Language')),
      status: Option.getOrUndefined(frontmatterField(wefts, 'Status')),
      priority: Option.getOrUndefined(frontmatterField(wefts, 'Priority')),
    }),
  )
}

~

`frontmatterField` reads a `key: value` field's value by name — the `Target` (or `Package`) a `{Tangle}` section writes to, the `Language` its sections inherit, or the `Status` and `Priority` a roadmap reads — lifting the missing case to `Option` at the `.find`.

=>

const frontmatterField = (
  wefts: ReadonlyArray<FrontmatterWeft>,
  key: string,
): Option.Option<FrontmatterValueToken> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(wefts.find((w) => w.key !== undefined && w.key.value === key)),
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((w) => w.value),
  )

## Assembling the document

`makeDocument` assembles the final `LoomDocument` from the folded frontmatter, preamble, and sections, building the frontmatter block through `makeFrontmatter`. `documentSpan` spans the whole, from the first node to the last. A document needs no `lang` Warp: a section without one inherits the workspace's primary language, so an absent declaration is no fault.

=>

const documentSpan = (db: DocumentBuilder): Position => {
  const all: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly position: Position }> = [
    ...db.frontmatter,
    ...db.preamble,
    ...db.sections,
  ]
  return all.length === 0
    ? { start: { line: 1, offset: 0 }, end: { line: 1, offset: 0 } }
    : { start: all[0].position.start, end: all[all.length - 1].position.end }
}

const makeDocument = (db: DocumentBuilder): LoomDocument => {
  const position = documentSpan(db)
  return LoomDocumentSchema.make({
    position,
    source: sourceOf(db.frontmatter, db.preamble, db.sections),
    health: okHealth,
    frontmatter: Option.getOrUndefined(makeFrontmatter(db.frontmatter)),
    preamble: db.preamble,
    sections: db.sections,
  })
}

## Recovering to an empty document

Some failures leave the chain with no tree to fold. `emptyDocument` is the recovery: an empty document — no preamble, no sections — whose root health carries one error diagnostic at the document's span. A caller that cannot produce a real document returns this one. So a `.loom` always parses to a tree, and the error rides in that tree's own health rather than being thrown.

`emptyDocumentFor` is the mixed-terminator case. The parse chain cannot pin a mixed line terminator to any node, so it raises `MixedEOL`. `emptyDocumentFor` names the two conventions and the lines they sit on, then builds the empty document from that message. `eolName` gives each convention — `LF`, `CRLF`, or `CR` — a name a person reads.

=>

export const emptyDocument = (text: string, message: string): LoomDocument => {
  const position: Position = {
    start: { line: 1, offset: 0 },
    end: { line: 1, offset: text.length },
  }
  return {
    type: 'LoomDocument',
    position,
    source: text,
    health: {
      status: 'error',
      diagnostics: [{ message, position, severity: 'error' }],
    },
    preamble: [],
    sections: [],
  }
}

export const emptyDocumentFor = (text: string, err: MixedEOL): LoomDocument =>
  emptyDocument(
    text,
    `Mixed line terminators. Line ${err.primaryLine} has ${eolName(err.primary)}, but line ${err.foundLine} has ${eolName(err.found)}. Pick one and stick with it.`,
  )

const eolName = (kind: 'lf' | 'crlf' | 'cr'): string => {
  switch (kind) {
    case 'lf':
      return 'LF (Unix)'
    case 'crlf':
      return 'CRLF (Windows)'
    case 'cr':
      return 'CR (classic Mac)'
  }
}

## What this module draws on

Effect supplies `Stream` to gather the weft stream, `Array` to group and fold it, `Data` to name the two openers, and `Match`, `Option`, `Context`, and `Layer` besides. The container schemas come from `LoomAst` and the frontmatter value token from `LoomTokens`; `Position` and `okHealth` from the foundation; the `MixedEOL` type from the line scanner; and the weft types from `Weft`. The fault catalog lends `faulty`, the `LoomFault` type, and the faults raised here. `Weft` lends `boxOf`, which reads the box a weft carries, and `LoomTokens` lends `boxKind`, `isPointingKind`, and `chapterKind`, which tell a box that points somewhere and a chapter box among them, `openingFence`, which tells a fence from a label, and the title token a label becomes.

=>

import { Array, Context, Data, Effect, Layer, Match, Option, Stream, pipe } from 'effect'
import {
  LoomDocumentSchema,
  LoomFrontmatterSchema,
  LoomHeadingSchema,
  LoomSectionSchema,
  type LoomDocument,
  type LoomFrontmatter,
  type LoomHeading,
  type LoomSection,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import {
  boxKind,
  type BoxToken,
  chapterKind,
  conclusionKind,
  type FrontmatterValueToken,
  isPointingKind,
  type HeadingTitleToken,
  HeadingTitleTokenSchema,
  openingFence,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import { okHealth, type Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { MixedEOL } from './LineRanges'
import {
  ChapterBoxBesideCode,
  ConclusionOutsideTopic,
  SecondConclusion,
  DividerAgainstCode,
  faulty,
  HeadingAgainstCode,
  type LoomFault,
  UnclosedBox,
  UnclosedFence,
  UnnamedBox,
} from './LoomFault'
import {
  boxOf,
  type DividerWeft,
  type FenceWeft,
  type FrontmatterWeft,
  type HeadingWeft,
  type LoomWeft,
  type PreambleWeft,
  type ProseWeft,
  type SectionBodyWeft,
  type TocWeft,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'

